Linux network 1.00
==================

RISC OS on Linux's network, from Configure.

RISC OS on Linux (the UTM and Raspberry Pi images) uses Linux's network:
a small Linux program, rol-netd, sets up the network interfaces. This
plug-in tells rol-netd what to do and shows what it is doing.

Connection
  Use        Automatic: the wired network if it has a cable plugged in,
             otherwise Wi-Fi if a network is chosen.
             Wired or Wi-Fi: only that one. Off: no network.
  Address    Automatic (DHCP), or Manual: the IP address, netmask,
             gateway and name servers (DNS: up to three, separated by
             commas or spaces).
  Host name  The machine's name (Linux's, and Inet$HostName).

Wi-Fi (on a Raspberry Pi 4, 5, 500 or CM4/CM5; the Pi 400's Wi-Fi chip
isn't supported)
  Network    The network's name. Scan... lists the networks in range:
             choose one from the menu.
  Password   The network's password (WPA or WPA2 Personal), or nothing for
             an open network. WPA3-only networks aren't supported yet.
  Country    Two letters (GB, US, DE...), for the radio channels allowed.

Status shows the interface in use and its link, the address and where it
came from, the gateway and name servers, the Wi-Fi network and its signal,
the traffic, the MAC address, and what rol-netd is doing. It updates every
two seconds.

Set (or Return) saves the settings and closes the window; Set with ADJUST
saves them and leaves it open. Cancel closes it; Cancel with ADJUST puts
back the settings saved. The settings take effect at once, and are kept
in the Linux file /riscos/net/config (with the Wi-Fi password, in plain
text: anyone who can read the SD card can read it).

RISC OS's Resolver is given the name servers (Inet$Resolvers) when RISC
OS starts and when they change while this window is open.

The icon is RISC OS Open's Network icon (InetSetup; Apache 2.0 licence)
with a badge.

